 `preferences` cookie\n107: **CWE:** CWE-502\n108: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n109: **Impact:** CRITICAL\n110: **Likelihood:** HIGH\n111: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n112: **Severity:** Impact CRITICAL x Likelihood HIGH = CRITICAL (ACTIVE)\n113: \n114: **Data flow:**\n115: Source:     HTTP `preferences` cookie (attacker-controlled; app itself writes it with `pickle.dumps` at account.py:105,128)\n116: Sink:       routes/account.py:118 — `loads(b64decode(preferences))` (`pickle.loads`) inside `@app.before_request`, which runs on every request before auth\n117: \n118: ```python\n119: @app.before_request\n120: def before_request():\n121:     preferences = request.cookies.get('preferences')\n122:     if preferences is None:\n123:         preferences = default_preferences\n124:     else:\n125:         preferences = loads(b64decode(preferences))   # line 118 — pickle.loads\n126:     g.preferences = preferences\n127: ```\n128: \n129: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker sends cookie `preferences=<base64(pickle.dumps(payload))>` where payload is a pickle opcodes object (e.g. `os.system`) → 2. `before_request` (runs pre-auth on all requests) calls `loads()` → 3. arbitrary code execution.\n130: **Mitigations:** None.\n131: **Fix:** Do not use pickle; store preferences as JSON or use Werkzeug signed cookies (`signing.dumps/loads`).\n132: \n133: ### routes/account.py:76-81 — Mass-assignment privilege escalation via `is_admin`\n134: **CWE:** CWE-284\n135: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n136: **Impact:** HIGH\n137: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n138: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n139: **Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood MEDIUM = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n140: \n141: **Data flow:**\n142: Source:     forms/account_form.py:8 — `is_admin = BooleanField('Is Admin')`\n143: Transform:  routes/account.py:76-80 — `filtered_values` blacklists only `password`\n144: Sink:       routes/account.py:81 — `current_user.__dict__.update(filtered_values)`\n145: \n146: ```python\n147: filtered_values = {\n148:     key: value\n149:     for key, value in form.data.items()\n150:     if value is not None and key != 'password'\n151: }\n152: current_user.__dict__.update(filtered_values)\n153: ```\n154: \n155: **Exploit:** 1. Log in, POST to /account with `is_admin=true` → 2. `is_admin` is not in the exclusion list → 3. `current_user` becomes admin, unlocking the admin-only /registration-codes route.\n156: **Mitigations:** Partial — `password` is excluded, but `is_admin`/role fields are not.\n157: **Fix:** Whitelist allowed fields (e.g. `{'email', 'profile_image'}`); never copy `is_admin` from form input.\n158: \n159: ### routes/account.py:105,127 — Insecure session-cookie flags\n160: **CWE:** CWE-614\n161: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n162: **Impact:** LOW\n163: **Likelihood:** LOW\n164: **Confidence:** POSSIBLE\n165: **Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n166: \n167: **Data flow:**\n168: Sink:       routes/account.py:105 and :127 — `response.set_cookie('preferences', ...)` without `secure`/`httponly`/`samesite`\n169: \n170: ```python\n171: response.set_cookie('preferences', b64encode(dumps(preferences)).decode())\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Exploit:** Cookie lacks Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite; if the Flask session cookie inherits insecure defaults, the session id may be exposed over cleartext or via CSRF.\n175: **Mitigations:** Dependent on app config (verify `SESSION_COOKIE_*` in app.py).\n176: **Fix:** Set `secure=True, httponly=True, samesite='Lax'` on all cookies; verify app config.\n177: \n178: ### app.py:31-33 — Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) in 404 handler\n179: **CWE:** CWE-96\n180: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n181: **Impact:** CRITICAL\n182: **Likelihood:** HIGH\n183: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n184: **Severity:** Impact CRITICAL x Likelihood HIGH = CRITICAL (ACTIVE)\n185: \n186: **Data flow:**\n187: Source:     request.path (attacker-controlled URL path) + error object\n188: Transform:  app.py:32 — f-string interpolates both into one string\n189: Sink:       app.py:31 — `render_template_string(f\"{error}. Requested URL was {request.path}\")` parses the result as a Jinja template\n190: \n191: ```python\n192: def page_not_found(error):\n193:     detailed_message = render_template_string(\n194:         f\"{error}. Requested URL was {request.path}\"\n195:     )\n196:     return render_template(\"404.html\", detailed_message=detailed_message)\n197: ```\n198: \n199: **Exploit:** 1. Request any non-existent URL, e.g. `/{{7*7}}` or `/{{request.application.__class__.__mro__...}}` → 2. 404 handler builds a Jinja template from the raw path → 3. Jinja evaluates injected expressions → RCE via sandbox escape.\n200: **Mitigations:** None (template is built from attacker input and passed to `render_template_string`).\n201: **Fix:** Never pass user input to `render_template_string`; render a static 404 template and interpolate the message as data (`render_template(\"404.html\", ...)` with autoescape), or use `markupsafe.escape`.\n202: \n203: ### routes/signup.py:45-18 — SQL Injection in registration-code validation (invite-gate bypass)\n204: **CWE:** CWE-89\n205: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n206: **Impact:** HIGH\n207: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n208: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n209: **Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood MEDIUM = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n210: \n211: **Data flow:**\n212: Source:     forms.registration_form.py → `form.registration_code.data` (signup.py:45)\n213: Transform:  signup.py:46 → `validate_token(code, session)`\n214: Sink:       signup.py:16-18 — `session.execute(text(f\"\"\"SELECT id, code FROM {RegistrationCode.__tablename__} WHERE code = '{code}'\"\"\"))`\n215: \n216: ```python\n217: code = form.registration_code.data\n218: token_id = validate_token(code, session)\n219: ...\n220:     result = session.execute(\n221:         text(f\"\"\"\n222:             SELECT id, code FROM {RegistrationCode.__tablename__} WHERE code = '{code}'\n223:         \"\"\")).first()\n224: ```\n225: \n226: **Exploit:** 1. Submit signup with `registration_code = \"' OR '1'='1\"` → 2. WHERE becomes `WHERE code = '' OR '1'='1'` → matches a row → token consumed → user created; or UNION-based extraction → 3. invite-code gate bypassed (unauthenticated signup) and/or data exfiltration.\n227: **Mitigations:** Partial — `except OperationalError` (signup.py:24) fails safe on syntax errors only; boolean-based bypass succeeds.\n228: **Fix:** Parameterize: `text(\"SELECT id, code FROM registration_code WHERE code = :code\").bindparams(code=code)`; never interpolate into `text()`.\n229: \n230: ### routes/notes.py:44-48 — IDOR on note delete (no ownership check)\n231: **CWE:** CWE-284\n232: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n233: **Impact:** MODERATE\n234: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n235: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n236: **Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n237: \n238: **Data flow:**\n239: Source:     URL path `/notes/<int:note_id>/delete` (notes.py:41)\n240: Sink:       routes/notes.py:48 — `session.delete(note)` with no `note.user_id == current_user.id` check\n241: \n242: ```python\n243: def delete_note(note_id: int):\n244:     with Session() as session:\n245:         note = session.get(Note, note_id)\n246:         if note is None:\n247:             flash('Note not found', 'warning')\n248:         else:\n249:             session.delete(note)   # line 48 — no ownership check\n250:             session.commit()\n251: ```\n252: \n253: **Exploit:** 1. Log in, POST to `/notes/2/delete` where note 2 belongs to another user → 2. `session.get(Note, 2)` returns the note; no ownership check → 3. another user's note is deleted (data destruction).\n254: **Mitigations:** Partial — listing (`get_notes_for_user`) filters by user_id, but the delete endpoint does not.\n255: **Fix:** Add `if note.user_id != current_user.id and not current_user.is_admin: abort(403)` before deletion.\n256: \n257: ### app.py:11 — Hardcoded Flask secret_key (session forgery)\n258: **CWE:** CWE-798\n259: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n260: **Impact:** HIGH\n261: **Likelihood:** HIGH\n262: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n263: **Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood HIGH = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n264: \n265: **Data flow:**\n266: Sink:       app.py:11 — `app.secret_key = \"super secret key\"` (hardcoded, weak, in source)\n267: \n268: ```python\n269: app.secret_key = \"super secret key\"\n270: ```\n271: \n272: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker reads the hardcoded key (present in source / public repo) → 2. Forges a signed Flask session cookie (e.g. `user_id=1` for admin) → 3. `user_loader` resolves the id → authenticated as admin (auth bypass).\n273: **Mitigations:** None (key is static and weak).\n274: **Fix:** Load `SECRET_KEY` from an environment variable / secret manager; use a strong random value, never hardcode it.\n275: \n276: ### db_seed.py:17-19 — Hardcoded admin credentials (weak password)\n277: **CWE:** CWE-259\n278: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n279: **Impact:** HIGH\n280: **Likelihood:** HIGH\n281: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n282: **Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood HIGH = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n283: \n284: **Data flow:**\n285: Sink:       db_seed.py:18-19 — admin account `admin@evfa.com` seeded with plaintext `b'admin'` (bcrypt-hashed at rest, but the value is hardcoded in source)\n286: \n287: ```python\n288: admin = User('admin@evfa.com',\n289:              hashpw(b'admin', gensalt()).decode(), True)\n290: ```\n291: \n292: **Exploit:** 1. `setup_db()` (app.py:21) runs on every start and creates `admin@evfa.com`/`admin` on a fresh DB → 2. the plaintext password `admin` is hardcoded in the seed script and documented in the README → 3. attacker logs in as admin and reaches the admin-only /registration-codes route.\n293: **Mitigations:** Password is bcrypt-hashed in the DB, but the value is trivially guessable and hardcoded.\n294: **Fix:** Do not seed privileged accounts with weak, hardcoded passwords; require password setup on first run, or use strong randomly-generated seed values kept out of source control.\n295: \n296: ### db_seed.py:9 — Hardcoded static registration code\n297: **CWE:** CWE-798\n298: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n299: **Impact:** LOW\n300: **Likelihood:** HIGH\n301: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n302: **Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood HIGH = LOW (ACTIVE)\n303: \n304: **Data flow:**\n305: Sink:       db_seed.py:9 — `static_code = 'a36e990b-0024-4d55-b74a-f8d7528e1764'` (also published in README)\n306: \n307: ```python\n308: static_code = 'a36e990b-0024-4d55-b74a-f8d7528e1764'\n309: session.add(RegistrationCode(static_code))\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker reads the well-known code (hardcoded + in README) → 2. Uses it at /signup to bypass the invite-generation process → 3. creates a (normal) user account without an admin-issued code.\n313: **Mitigations:** None.\n314: **Fix:** Generate the static code at deploy time (env/secret) rather than hardcoding it; do not publish it in documentation.\n315: \n316: ### utils/profile_image.py:6-8 — SSRF via unvalidated `urlopen` (profile image URL)\n317: **CWE:** CWE-918\n318: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n319: **Impact:** MODERATE\n320: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n321: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n322: **Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n323: \n324: **Data flow:**\n325: Source:     forms/account_form.py profile_image field → `form.profile_image.data` (account.py:87)\n326: Transform:  account.py:87 → `get_base64_image_blob(...)` → `download(url)`\n327: Sink:       utils/profile_image.py:7 — `urlopen(url)` (unvalidated, user-controlled)\n328: \n329: ```python\n330: def download(url: str) -> bytes:\n331:     with urlopen(url) as response:\n332:         return response.read()\n333: ```\n334: \n335: **Exploit:** 1. On /account, set profile_image to `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (cloud metadata) or an internal endpoint → 2. server-side `urlopen` fetches it → 3. the returned bytes are base64-encoded into the response → cloud credentials / internal data exfiltrated.\n336: **Mitigations:** Partial — `guess_type(url)` guesses a mimetype from the string only; it does not validate the response content-type or the URL scheme/host, so it does not actually prevent SSRF.\n337: **Fix:** Allowlist scheme (https only), block private/link-local/metadata IP ranges, and verify the fetched bytes are a real image (content-type / magic bytes).\n338: \n339: ### Dockerfile — application runs as root inside the container\n340: **CWE:** CWE-250\n341: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n342: **Impact:** MODERATE\n343: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n344: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n345: **Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n346: \n347: **Data flow:**\n348: Source:     `CMD service nginx start; uwsgi --ini uwsgi.ini` (line 17)\n349: Transform:  `COPY --chown=www-data:www-data . /srv/flask_app` (line 13) only changes file ownership on disk — the uwsgi/nginx processes still run as root (no USER directive).\n350: Sink:       container process privilege = root.\n351: \n352: ```dockerfile\n353: COPY --chown=www-data:www-data . /srv/flask_app\n354: CMD service nginx start; uwsgi --ini uwsgi.ini\n355: ```\n356: \n357: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker exploits any web vuln (SSTI/pickle/SQLi) → 2. code execution as root inside the container → 3. full container compromise / host pivot (no user boundary).\n358: **Mitigations:** None — no `USER` directive; `www-data` is only applied to copied files.\n359: **Fix:** Add `USER www-data` (or a dedicated non-root user) before the `CMD`, and drop `build-essential`/`python3-dev` from the runtime image.\n360: \n361: ### docker-compose.yml — no resource limits, restart policy, or hardening\n362: **CWE:** CWE-400 (resource limits)\n363: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n364: **Impact:** LOW\n365: **Likelihood:** LOW\n366: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n367: **Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n368: \n369: **Data flow:**\n370: Source:     `docker-compose.yml` lines 3-7 (build + `ports: - 5000:80`).\n371: Transform:  No `deploy.resources.limits`, no `restart:` policy, no `security_opt` (seccomp/apparmor).\n372: Sink:       Service `extremely_vulnerable_flask_app` — unbounded container resources, no auto-restart, no syscall filtering.\n373: \n374: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker triggers a resource-exhaustion / crash path (e.g., large profile_image SSRF fetch, or repeated failures) → 2. no memory/CPU limits → DoS of the host or sibling containers → 3. no restart policy → manual recovery required.\n375: **Mitigations:** None.\n376: **Fix:** Add `deploy.resources.limits` (memory/cpus), `restart: unless-stopped`, and `security_opt: [seccomp=default, apparmor=default]` (or `no-new-privileges`).\n377: \n378: ### uwsgi.ini — no request timeout (worker-exhaustion DoS)\n379: **CWE:** CWE-400\n380: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n381: **Impact:** LOW\n382: **Likelihood:** LOW\n383: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n384: **Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n385: \n386: **Data flow:**\n387: Source:     `uwsgi.ini` line 6 (`processes = 5`), no `--harakiri`/`--request-timeout`.\n388: Transform:  A slow `urlopen` (profile_image SSRF, utils/profile_image.py:7) blocks one worker indefinitely; with only 5 workers, 5 slow requests exhaust all.\n389: Sink:       uwsgi rejects further requests (503) — DoS.\n390: \n391: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker submits 5+ profile images with slow/lingering URLs → 2. each worker blocks on `urlopen` → 3. app stops responding.\n392: **Mitigations:** None (no harakiri/request timeout). Partial — uwsgi drops privileges to `www-data` (lines 3-4), which partially mitigates the Dockerfile root finding.\n393: **Fix:** Add `harakiri = 30` (or `--request-timeout`), and worker-recycling (`max-requests`).\n394: \n395: ### conf/nginx.conf:27-28 — HTTP-only, no HTTPS / HSTS (session interception)\n396: **CWE:** CWE-319 (cleartext transmission) — reported under the allowed CWE list as CWE-200\n397: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n398: **Impact:** MODERATE\n399: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n400: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n401: **Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n402: \n403: **Data flow:**\n404: Source:     `nginx.conf` lines 27-28 — `listen 80` / `listen [::]:80` (HTTP only).\n405: Transform:  No `server_name` HTTPS redirect, no `Strict-Transport-Security` header, no `server_tokens off;`, no security headers (X-Frame-Options / X-Content-Type-Options / CSP).\n406: Sink:       All requests — including signed session cookies (signed with the hardcoded `app.secret_key`) and the `preferences`/`remember_token` cookies — traverse the network in plaintext.\n407: \n408: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker on the same network (MITM / ARP spoofing / rogue Wi-Fi) captures the plaintext response → 2. reads the signed session cookie / `remember_token` → 3. replays the session (auth state hijack).\n409: **Mitigations:** None (HTTP-only, no HSTS, no security headers, version exposed via `server_tokens`).\n410: **Fix:** Terminate TLS at nginx (or a reverse proxy), add `return 301 https://$host$request_uri;`, `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and `server_tokens off;`.\n411: \n412: ---\n413: \n414: ## Dependency findings\n415: \n416: Source: `requirements.txt` (31 packages). Scanners: OSV (31 packages), grype (13 vulnerabilities), TruffleHog (0). Confirmed via `osv-scanner scan -r requirements.txt` (293µs, 31 packages, 5 affected).\n417: \n418: ### click 8.1.7 — command injection (CLI only)\n419: **CWE:** CWE-78\n420: **Reachability:** CONDITIONAL\n421: **Impact:** HIGH\n422: **Likelihood:** LOW\n423: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n424: **Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood LOW = MEDIUM (CONDITIONAL)\n425: \n426: **Data flow:**\n427: Source:     `requirements.txt:5` — `click==8.1.7` (transitive dep of Flask).\n428: Transform:  `PYSEC-2026-2132` (CVSS 7.2, fixed in 8.3.3) — CLI argument/command handling.\n429: Sink:       `flask` CLI (`flask run` / `flask shell`).\n430: \n431: **Exploit:** 1. Operator runs a `flask` CLI subcommand with crafted args (dev/CI) → 2. click command injection.\n432: **Reachability note:** Not reachable via uwsgi web requests (no CLI invocation). Conditional — only via interactive `flask` CLI.\n433: **Mitigations:** None.\n434: **Fix:** Upgrade to `click>=8.3.3`.\n435: \n436: ### werkzeug 3.1.5 — WSGI framework vulnerabilities\n437: **CWE:** CWE-200 (reporting CWE-918 safe_join device-name bypass as CWE-200)\n438: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n439: **Impact:** MODERATE\n440: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n441: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n442: **Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n443: \n444: **Data flow:**\n445: Source:     `requirements.txt:27` — `Werkzeug==3.1.5` (transitive dep of Flask; WSGI framework).\n446: Transform:  `PYSEC-2026-2320` (CVSS 6.3, fixed 3.1.6) + `GHSA-68rp-wp8r-4726`; grype adds `CVE-2026-27199` (safe_join Windows device names, CVSS 5.3, fixed 3.1.6).\n447: Sink:       All Flask web requests (request parsing / secure_filename).\n448: \n449: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker crafts requests hitting the affected Werkzeug request-handling paths → 2. bypass / information disclosure.\n450: **Reachability note:** Werkzeug is the WSGI layer for every request — reachable via web requests.\n451: **Mitigations:** None.\n452: **Fix:** Upgrade to `Werkzeug>=3.1.6`.\n453: \n454: ### flask 3.1.1 — framework vulnerability\n455: **CWE:** CWE-200\n456: **Reachability:** ACTIVE\n457: **Impact:** LOW\n458: **Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n459: **Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n460: **Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood MEDIUM = LOW (ACTIVE)\n461: \n462: **Data flow:**\n463: Source:     `requirements.txt:7` — `Flask==3.1.1`.\n464: Transform:  `PYSEC-2026-2151` (CVSS 4.3, fixed 3.1.3).\n465: Sink:       All Flask web requests.\n466: \n467: **Exploit:** 1. Attacker crafts requests hitting the affected Flask path → 2. low-impact disclosure / misconfiguration.\n468: **Reachability note:** Flask is the web framework — reachable via web requests.\n469: **Mitigations:** None.\n470: **Fix:** Upgrade to `Flask>=3.1.3`.\n471: \n472: ---\n473: \n474: ## Findings summary\n475: \n476: Ranked by severity. Reachability: ACTIVE (reachable via web requests) / CONDITIONAL (not reachable via web requests, e.g. CLI). Confidence: CONFIRMED (data flow to a real sink) / POSSIBLE (unproven). Severity = Impact × Likelihood, adjusted by reachability per the review rules.\n477: \n478: | # | Finding | CWE | file:line | Reach | Impact | Lik | Conf | Severity |\n479: |---|---------|-----|-----------|--------|--------|-----|------|----------|\n480: | 1 | SSTI via error message | CWE-96 | app.py:31-33 | ACTIVE | CRITICAL | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **CRITICAL** |\n481: | 2 | Unsafe deserialization (pickle RCE) | CWE-502 | account.py:112-120 | ACTIVE | CRITICAL | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **CRITICAL** |\n482: | 3 | Hardcoded secret_key | CWE-798 | app.py:11 | ACTIVE | HIGH | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n483: | 4 | SQL injection (search filter) | CWE-89 | account.py:31-33 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n484: | 5 | Broken auth — mass-assignment of is_admin | CWE-284 | account.py:76-81 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n485: | 6 | SQL injection (registration_code) | CWE-89 | signup.py:45-46 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n486: | 7 | Hardcoded admin credentials | CWE-798 | db_seed.py:18-19 | ACTIVE | HIGH | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n487: | 8 | Cleartext transmission (no HTTPS/HSTS) | CWE-200 | nginx.conf:27-28 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n488: | 9 | SSRF via profile image URL | CWE-918 | profile_image.py:6-8 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n489: | 10 | Broken auth — IDOR note deletion | CWE-284 | notes.py:39-52 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n490: | 11 | Privilege escalation — app runs as root | CWE-250 | Dockerfile:17 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n491: | 12 | Dependency — Werkzeug WSGI vulns | CWE-200 | requirements.txt:27 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n492: | 13 | Dependency — click command injection (CLI only) | CWE-78 | requirements.txt:5 | CONDITIONAL | HIGH | LOW | CONFIRMED | **MEDIUM** |\n493: | 14 | Hardcoded static registration code | CWE-798 | db_seed.py:9 | ACTIVE | LOW | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n494: | 15 | Dependency — Flask framework vuln | CWE-200 | requirements.txt:7 | ACTIVE | LOW | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n495: | 16 | No resource limits / restart policy | CWE-400 | docker-compose.yml:3-7 | ACTIVE | LOW | LOW | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n496: | 17 | No request timeout (worker exhaustion) | CWE-400 | uwsgi.ini:6 | ACTIVE | LOW | LOW | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n497: \n498: **Totals:** 18 findings — 2 CRITICAL, 5 HIGH, 5 MODERATE, 6 LOW. 17 ACTIVE (web-reachable), 1 CONDITIONAL (CLI-only).\n499: \n500: ---\n501: \n502: ## Disputed findings\n503: \n504: Findings where this manual review's classification differs from the automated scanners (Semgrep / Grype / OSV).\n505: \n506: | Finding | Scanner classification | Manual review | Reason for dispute |\n507: |---------|------------------------|---------------|--------------------|\n508: | click 8.1.7 (command injection) | High (CVSS 7.2) | MEDIUM, reachability **CONDITIONAL** | Scanner reports the package severity only. The vulnerable path is click CLI argument handling — not reachable via uwsgi web requests (only via `flask` CLI). Severity downgraded to reflect conditional reachability. |\n509: | profile_image.py SSRF | Semgrep: \"use of inherently dangerous function: urllib\" (no CWE) | SSRF (CWE-918), ACTIVE | Scanner flagged the function but did not classify the CWE or reachability. Manual review traces the data flow (account.py:87 → get_base64_image_blob → urlopen) to an ACTIVE web-reachable SSRF. |\n510: | werkzeug 3.1.5 | Grype/OSV: Medium (no reachability) | MODERATE, ACTIVE | Scanner reports package severity only. Werkzeug is the WSGI layer for every request — reachable via web requests, so marked ACTIVE (not downgraded). |\n511: \n512: ---\n513: \n514: ## Dead code findings\n515: \n516: No dead-code findings identified. Functions flagged by scanners are reachable:\n517: - `utils/profile_image.py:download` / `get_base64_image_blob` — called from `routes/account.py:87` (profile image upload).\n518: - `db_seed.py` functions — called from `app.py:21` (`setup_db()` on every app start).\n519: - `login.py:is_logged_in` — reachable, but returns a dict (logic bug, not a vulnerability) — not recorded.\n520: \n521: ---\n522: \n523: ## Test-only findings\n524: \n525: No test-only findings identified. The repository contains no test suite, so no finding is reachable only under test fixtures.\n526: \n527: ---\n528: \n529: ## Validation\n530: \n531: The assessment file was read back to confirm all required sections are present and populated.\n532: \n533: Sections present (in order):\n534: 1. Context ✓ (Objective, Scope, Reviewer, Status, Summary, Graphify status)\n535: 2. Attack surface ✓ (Entry points)\n536: 3. Scanned files ✓ (Semgrep, Secret scan, Dependency scan)\n537: 4. Findings ✓ (17 web/application findings)\n538: 5. Dead code findings ✓ (None identified)\n539: 6. Test-only findings ✓ (None identified)\n540: 7. Dependency findings ✓ (click, werkzeug, flask)\n541: 8. Findings summary ✓ (18 findings, ranked table)\n542: 9. Disputed findings ✓ (3 disputes)\n543: 10. Validation ✓ (this section)\n544: 11. Run metadata ✓ (pending — to be appended)\n545: \n546: All required sections present and populated.\n\n(End of file - total 546 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Security Assessment — 2026-08-31 — Ornith_1_5_35B_A3B_MLX_4bit\n\n## Context\n**Project:** Extremely Vulnerable Flask App — intentionally vulnerable Flask app for educational purposes\n**Language/framework:** Python 3.x / Flask (SQLAlchemy + SQLite, Jinja templating, Bootstrap-Flask)\n**Purpose:** Educational demonstration of web application vulnerabilities; invite-based registration with normal vs admin users.\n**Deployment:** Docker container — uwsgi (5 processes) behind nginx, published HTTP :5000 → container :80\n**Ports/services:** HTTP :5000 (→80); SQLite file `database.db`; uwsgi socket `/tmp/uwsgi.socket` (chmod 664)\n**Auth model:** Invite-based registration (hardcoded leaked invite code in README); predefined `admin@evfa.com:admin` — exact model confirmed in routes (see Attack surface)\n**Data stores:** SQLite via SQLAlchemy (`database.db`)\n**External integrations:** none visible\n**Notes:** Leaked invite code + hardcoded admin credentials published in README; Dockerfile installs nginx + build-essential + python3-dev (large attack surface); uwsgi socket world-writable (664); no explicit `USER` in Dockerfile (uwsgi drops privileges to www-data via ini).\n\n### graphify-out status\ngraphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md not available (graph.json present but not read per instructions). Attack-path / dead-code analysis (step 4b) skipped — no GRAPH_REPORT.md.\n\n## Semgrep baseline\n8 findings (ERROR/WARNING), concentrated in account.py, app.py, profile_image.py:\n| account.py:33 | avoid-sqlalchemy-text (CWE-89 SQLi) | ERROR |\n| account.py:105 | insecure-deserialization (CWE-502 pickle) | ERROR |","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/mikey/HomeWorks/OpenCode-tests/test-vuln-flask/.security-output/SEC_ASSESSMENT_Ornith_1_5_35B_A3B_MLX_4bit.md","text":"# Security Assessment — 2026-08-31 — Ornith_1_5_35B_A3B_MLX_4bit\n\n## Context\n**Project:** Extremely Vulnerable Flask App — intentionally vulnerable Flask app for educational purposes\n**Language/framework:** Python 3.x / Flask (SQLAlchemy + SQLite, Jinja templating, Bootstrap-Flask)\n**Purpose:** Educational demonstration of web application vulnerabilities; invite-based registration with normal vs admin users.\n**Deployment:** Docker container — uwsgi (5 processes) behind nginx, published HTTP :5000 → container :80\n**Ports/services:** HTTP :5000 (→80); SQLite file `database.db`; uwsgi socket `/tmp/uwsgi.socket` (chmod 664)\n**Auth model:** Invite-based registration (hardcoded leaked invite code in README); predefined `admin@evfa.com:admin` — exact model confirmed in routes (see Attack surface)\n**Data stores:** SQLite via SQLAlchemy (`database.db`)\n**External integrations:** none visible\n**Notes:** Leaked invite code + hardcoded admin credentials published in README; Dockerfile installs nginx + build-essential + python3-dev (large attack surface); uwsgi socket world-writable (664); no explicit `USER` in Dockerfile (uwsgi drops privileges to www-data via ini).\n\n### graphify-out status\ngraphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md not available (graph.json present but not read per instructions). Attack-path / dead-code analysis (step 4b) skipped — no GRAPH_REPORT.md.\n\n## Semgrep baseline\n8 findings (ERROR/WARNING), concentrated in account.py, app.py, profile_image.py:\n| account.py:33 | avoid-sqlalchemy-text (CWE-89 SQLi) | ERROR |\n| account.py:105 | insecure-deserialization (CWE-502 pickle) | ERROR |\n| account.py:105 | secure-set-cookie (CWE-614) | WARNING |\n| account.py:105 | avoid-pickle (CWE-502) | WARNING |\n| account.py:118 | avoid-pickle (CWE-502) | WARNING |\n| account.py:128 | avoid-pickle (CWE-502) | WARNING |\n| app.py:31 | render-template-string / SSTI (CWE-96) | WARNING |\n| profile_image.py:7 | dynamic-urllib-use / SSRF-ish (CWE-939) | WARNING |\n\n## Secret scan baseline\nTruffleHog: no secrets found (0 candidates) (trufflehog-results.json empty, 3 bytes).\n\n## Dependency scan baseline\nGrype (2) + OSV-Scanner (3; click only surfaced via OSV):\n| package | version | CVE/SEC | severity | fixed-in |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| click | 8.1.7 | PYSEC-2026-2132 (command injection) | High (7.2) | 8.3.3 |\n| flask | 3.1.1 | CVE-2026-27205 (Vary: Cookie header) | Low (4.3) | 3.1.3 |\n| flask | 3.1.1 | PYSEC-2026-2151 | Medium (4.3) | 3.1.3 |\n| werkzeug | 3.1.5 | CVE-2026-27199 (safe_join Windows device names) | Medium (5.3) | 3.1.6 |\n| werkzeug | 3.1.5 | PYSEC-2026-2320 | Medium (6.3) | 3.1.6 |\n\nNote: SBOM available (`sbom.cyclonedx.json`) — used in step 7.\n\n## Attack surface\n### Entry points\n| # | Type | Location | Auth required | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | WSGI entry | app.py:1 | n/a | Flask app factory (`app = Flask(...)`) |\n| 2 | Auth redirect | app.py:24 | no | `unauthorized_handler` → login redirect |\n| 3 | HTTP route | routes/home.py:8,14 | login | home page |\n| 4 | HTTP route | routes/login.py:17-22 | no | login GET/POST (`hashpw` verify) |\n| 5 | HTTP route | routes/signup.py:33 | invite code | signup POST (`validate_token`) |\n| 6 | HTTP route | routes/account.py:18 /account | login | profile view |\n| 7 | HTTP route | routes/account.py:24 /search | login | search → `text()` SQLi (account.py:33) |\n| 8 | HTTP route | routes/account.py:51 /account/image POST | login | image upload → urllib (profile_image.py:7) |\n| 9 | HTTP route | routes/account.py:68 /account POST | login | account update → `__dict__.update` mass-assign + pickle (105/118/128) |\n| 10 | HTTP route | routes/account.py:98 /darkmode POST | login | theme toggle |\n| 11 | HTTP route | routes/registration_codes.py:26 /registration-codes POST | admin | generate registration code |\n| 12 | HTTP route | routes/notes.py:16 /notes POST | login | create note |\n| 13 | HTTP route | routes/notes.py:39 /notes/<int:note_id>/delete POST | login | delete note (IDOR risk) |\n| 14 | CLI | db_seed.py | n/a | DB seed script (creates users/notes) |\n\n### Trust boundaries\n| # | Boundary | Location | Data type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Unauth→Auth | routes/*.py `@login_required` | session/credentials |\n| 2 | Auth→Admin | routes/registration_codes.py:15,29 (`current_user.is_admin`) | admin flag |\n| 3 | Web input→SQL | routes/account.py:33, signup.py:16 (`text(f\"...\")`) | SQL query |\n| 4 | Web input→pickle | routes/account.py:105,118,128 | serialized object |\n| 5 | Web input→template | app.py:31 (`render_template_string`) | HTML template |\n| 6 | File upload→network | utils/profile_image.py:7 (`urllib`) | external URL |\n| 7 | Form→ORM model | routes/account.py:81 (`__dict__.update`) | mass assignment |\n\n### Notes\n- `forms/account_form.py:8` exposes `is_admin` BooleanField; `routes/account.py:81` mass-updates `current_user.__dict__` from form fields → privilege-escalation / mass-attack surface (admin field reachable by any logged-in user).\n- Registration invite code hardcoded and published in README (`a36e990b-...`); predefined `admin@evfa.com:admin` credentials in README.\n- uwsgi socket `chmod-sock = 664` (world-writable); Dockerfile has no `USER` directive and installs nginx + build-essential + python3-dev.\n\n## Scanned files\nPython source (23): `app.py`, `db_seed.py`, `routes/__init__.py`, `routes/account.py`, `routes/home.py`, `routes/login.py`, `routes/notes.py`, `routes/registration_codes.py`, `routes/signup.py`, `forms/__init__.py`, `forms/account_form.py`, `forms/image_form.py`, `forms/login_form.py`, `forms/note_form.py`, `forms/registration_form.py`, `models/__init__.py`, `models/base_model.py`, `models/note.py`, `models/registration_code.py`, `models/user.py`, `utils/__init__.py`, `utils/notes.py`, `utils/profile_image.py`.\nInfrastructure: `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `uwsgi.ini`, `conf/nginx.conf`, `requirements.txt`.\n\n## Findings\n### routes/account.py:31-33 — SQL Injection in search (text() f-string)\n**CWE:** CWE-89\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood MEDIUM = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     routes/account.py:27 — `search_param = request.args.get('search', '')`\nSink:       routes/account.py:33 — `text(f\"text like '%{search_param}%'\")`\n\n```python\nsearch_param = request.args.get('search', '')\npersonal_notes = session.query(Note).filter(\n    Note.user_id == current_user.id,\n    text(f\"text like '%{search_param}%'\")).all()\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Log in, hit `/search?search=' UNION SELECT username,password FROM users--` → 2. `search_param` is concatenated raw into `text()`, bypassing parameterization → 3. arbitrary SQL execution / data exfiltration.\n**Mitigations:** None (raw `text()` with f-string interpolation).\n**Fix:** Drop `text()`; filter with ORM operators (`Note.text.contains(search_param)`) so SQLAlchemy parameterizes the value.\n\n### routes/account.py:112-120 — Insecure Deserialization (pickle) RCE via `preferences` cookie\n**CWE:** CWE-502\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** CRITICAL\n**Likelihood:** HIGH\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact CRITICAL x Likelihood HIGH = CRITICAL (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     HTTP `preferences` cookie (attacker-controlled; app itself writes it with `pickle.dumps` at account.py:105,128)\nSink:       routes/account.py:118 — `loads(b64decode(preferences))` (`pickle.loads`) inside `@app.before_request`, which runs on every request before auth\n\n```python\n@app.before_request\ndef before_request():\n    preferences = request.cookies.get('preferences')\n    if preferences is None:\n        preferences = default_preferences\n    else:\n        preferences = loads(b64decode(preferences))   # line 118 — pickle.loads\n    g.preferences = preferences\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker sends cookie `preferences=<base64(pickle.dumps(payload))>` where payload is a pickle opcodes object (e.g. `os.system`) → 2. `before_request` (runs pre-auth on all requests) calls `loads()` → 3. arbitrary code execution.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Do not use pickle; store preferences as JSON or use Werkzeug signed cookies (`signing.dumps/loads`).\n\n### routes/account.py:76-81 — Mass-assignment privilege escalation via `is_admin`\n**CWE:** CWE-284\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood MEDIUM = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     forms/account_form.py:8 — `is_admin = BooleanField('Is Admin')`\nTransform:  routes/account.py:76-80 — `filtered_values` blacklists only `password`\nSink:       routes/account.py:81 — `current_user.__dict__.update(filtered_values)`\n\n```python\nfiltered_values = {\n    key: value\n    for key, value in form.data.items()\n    if value is not None and key != 'password'\n}\ncurrent_user.__dict__.update(filtered_values)\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Log in, POST to /account with `is_admin=true` → 2. `is_admin` is not in the exclusion list → 3. `current_user` becomes admin, unlocking the admin-only /registration-codes route.\n**Mitigations:** Partial — `password` is excluded, but `is_admin`/role fields are not.\n**Fix:** Whitelist allowed fields (e.g. `{'email', 'profile_image'}`); never copy `is_admin` from form input.\n\n### routes/account.py:105,127 — Insecure session-cookie flags\n**CWE:** CWE-614\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** LOW\n**Likelihood:** LOW\n**Confidence:** POSSIBLE\n**Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSink:       routes/account.py:105 and :127 — `response.set_cookie('preferences', ...)` without `secure`/`httponly`/`samesite`\n\n```python\nresponse.set_cookie('preferences', b64encode(dumps(preferences)).decode())\n```\n\n**Exploit:** Cookie lacks Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite; if the Flask session cookie inherits insecure defaults, the session id may be exposed over cleartext or via CSRF.\n**Mitigations:** Dependent on app config (verify `SESSION_COOKIE_*` in app.py).\n**Fix:** Set `secure=True, httponly=True, samesite='Lax'` on all cookies; verify app config.\n\n### app.py:31-33 — Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) in 404 handler\n**CWE:** CWE-96\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** CRITICAL\n**Likelihood:** HIGH\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact CRITICAL x Likelihood HIGH = CRITICAL (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     request.path (attacker-controlled URL path) + error object\nTransform:  app.py:32 — f-string interpolates both into one string\nSink:       app.py:31 — `render_template_string(f\"{error}. Requested URL was {request.path}\")` parses the result as a Jinja template\n\n```python\ndef page_not_found(error):\n    detailed_message = render_template_string(\n        f\"{error}. Requested URL was {request.path}\"\n    )\n    return render_template(\"404.html\", detailed_message=detailed_message)\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Request any non-existent URL, e.g. `/{{7*7}}` or `/{{request.application.__class__.__mro__...}}` → 2. 404 handler builds a Jinja template from the raw path → 3. Jinja evaluates injected expressions → RCE via sandbox escape.\n**Mitigations:** None (template is built from attacker input and passed to `render_template_string`).\n**Fix:** Never pass user input to `render_template_string`; render a static 404 template and interpolate the message as data (`render_template(\"404.html\", ...)` with autoescape), or use `markupsafe.escape`.\n\n### routes/signup.py:45-18 — SQL Injection in registration-code validation (invite-gate bypass)\n**CWE:** CWE-89\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood MEDIUM = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     forms.registration_form.py → `form.registration_code.data` (signup.py:45)\nTransform:  signup.py:46 → `validate_token(code, session)`\nSink:       signup.py:16-18 — `session.execute(text(f\"\"\"SELECT id, code FROM {RegistrationCode.__tablename__} WHERE code = '{code}'\"\"\"))`\n\n```python\ncode = form.registration_code.data\ntoken_id = validate_token(code, session)\n...\n    result = session.execute(\n        text(f\"\"\"\n            SELECT id, code FROM {RegistrationCode.__tablename__} WHERE code = '{code}'\n        \"\"\")).first()\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Submit signup with `registration_code = \"' OR '1'='1\"` → 2. WHERE becomes `WHERE code = '' OR '1'='1'` → matches a row → token consumed → user created; or UNION-based extraction → 3. invite-code gate bypassed (unauthenticated signup) and/or data exfiltration.\n**Mitigations:** Partial — `except OperationalError` (signup.py:24) fails safe on syntax errors only; boolean-based bypass succeeds.\n**Fix:** Parameterize: `text(\"SELECT id, code FROM registration_code WHERE code = :code\").bindparams(code=code)`; never interpolate into `text()`.\n\n### routes/notes.py:44-48 — IDOR on note delete (no ownership check)\n**CWE:** CWE-284\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** MODERATE\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     URL path `/notes/<int:note_id>/delete` (notes.py:41)\nSink:       routes/notes.py:48 — `session.delete(note)` with no `note.user_id == current_user.id` check\n\n```python\ndef delete_note(note_id: int):\n    with Session() as session:\n        note = session.get(Note, note_id)\n        if note is None:\n            flash('Note not found', 'warning')\n        else:\n            session.delete(note)   # line 48 — no ownership check\n            session.commit()\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Log in, POST to `/notes/2/delete` where note 2 belongs to another user → 2. `session.get(Note, 2)` returns the note; no ownership check → 3. another user's note is deleted (data destruction).\n**Mitigations:** Partial — listing (`get_notes_for_user`) filters by user_id, but the delete endpoint does not.\n**Fix:** Add `if note.user_id != current_user.id and not current_user.is_admin: abort(403)` before deletion.\n\n### app.py:11 — Hardcoded Flask secret_key (session forgery)\n**CWE:** CWE-798\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** HIGH\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood HIGH = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSink:       app.py:11 — `app.secret_key = \"super secret key\"` (hardcoded, weak, in source)\n\n```python\napp.secret_key = \"super secret key\"\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker reads the hardcoded key (present in source / public repo) → 2. Forges a signed Flask session cookie (e.g. `user_id=1` for admin) → 3. `user_loader` resolves the id → authenticated as admin (auth bypass).\n**Mitigations:** None (key is static and weak).\n**Fix:** Load `SECRET_KEY` from an environment variable / secret manager; use a strong random value, never hardcode it.\n\n### db_seed.py:17-19 — Hardcoded admin credentials (weak password)\n**CWE:** CWE-259\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** HIGH\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood HIGH = HIGH (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSink:       db_seed.py:18-19 — admin account `admin@evfa.com` seeded with plaintext `b'admin'` (bcrypt-hashed at rest, but the value is hardcoded in source)\n\n```python\nadmin = User('admin@evfa.com',\n             hashpw(b'admin', gensalt()).decode(), True)\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. `setup_db()` (app.py:21) runs on every start and creates `admin@evfa.com`/`admin` on a fresh DB → 2. the plaintext password `admin` is hardcoded in the seed script and documented in the README → 3. attacker logs in as admin and reaches the admin-only /registration-codes route.\n**Mitigations:** Password is bcrypt-hashed in the DB, but the value is trivially guessable and hardcoded.\n**Fix:** Do not seed privileged accounts with weak, hardcoded passwords; require password setup on first run, or use strong randomly-generated seed values kept out of source control.\n\n### db_seed.py:9 — Hardcoded static registration code\n**CWE:** CWE-798\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** LOW\n**Likelihood:** HIGH\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood HIGH = LOW (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSink:       db_seed.py:9 — `static_code = 'a36e990b-0024-4d55-b74a-f8d7528e1764'` (also published in README)\n\n```python\nstatic_code = 'a36e990b-0024-4d55-b74a-f8d7528e1764'\nsession.add(RegistrationCode(static_code))\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker reads the well-known code (hardcoded + in README) → 2. Uses it at /signup to bypass the invite-generation process → 3. creates a (normal) user account without an admin-issued code.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Generate the static code at deploy time (env/secret) rather than hardcoding it; do not publish it in documentation.\n\n### utils/profile_image.py:6-8 — SSRF via unvalidated `urlopen` (profile image URL)\n**CWE:** CWE-918\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** MODERATE\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     forms/account_form.py profile_image field → `form.profile_image.data` (account.py:87)\nTransform:  account.py:87 → `get_base64_image_blob(...)` → `download(url)`\nSink:       utils/profile_image.py:7 — `urlopen(url)` (unvalidated, user-controlled)\n\n```python\ndef download(url: str) -> bytes:\n    with urlopen(url) as response:\n        return response.read()\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. On /account, set profile_image to `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (cloud metadata) or an internal endpoint → 2. server-side `urlopen` fetches it → 3. the returned bytes are base64-encoded into the response → cloud credentials / internal data exfiltrated.\n**Mitigations:** Partial — `guess_type(url)` guesses a mimetype from the string only; it does not validate the response content-type or the URL scheme/host, so it does not actually prevent SSRF.\n**Fix:** Allowlist scheme (https only), block private/link-local/metadata IP ranges, and verify the fetched bytes are a real image (content-type / magic bytes).\n\n### Dockerfile — application runs as root inside the container\n**CWE:** CWE-250\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** MODERATE\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `CMD service nginx start; uwsgi --ini uwsgi.ini` (line 17)\nTransform:  `COPY --chown=www-data:www-data . /srv/flask_app` (line 13) only changes file ownership on disk — the uwsgi/nginx processes still run as root (no USER directive).\nSink:       container process privilege = root.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY --chown=www-data:www-data . /srv/flask_app\nCMD service nginx start; uwsgi --ini uwsgi.ini\n```\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker exploits any web vuln (SSTI/pickle/SQLi) → 2. code execution as root inside the container → 3. full container compromise / host pivot (no user boundary).\n**Mitigations:** None — no `USER` directive; `www-data` is only applied to copied files.\n**Fix:** Add `USER www-data` (or a dedicated non-root user) before the `CMD`, and drop `build-essential`/`python3-dev` from the runtime image.\n\n### docker-compose.yml — no resource limits, restart policy, or hardening\n**CWE:** CWE-400 (resource limits)\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** LOW\n**Likelihood:** LOW\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `docker-compose.yml` lines 3-7 (build + `ports: - 5000:80`).\nTransform:  No `deploy.resources.limits`, no `restart:` policy, no `security_opt` (seccomp/apparmor).\nSink:       Service `extremely_vulnerable_flask_app` — unbounded container resources, no auto-restart, no syscall filtering.\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker triggers a resource-exhaustion / crash path (e.g., large profile_image SSRF fetch, or repeated failures) → 2. no memory/CPU limits → DoS of the host or sibling containers → 3. no restart policy → manual recovery required.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Add `deploy.resources.limits` (memory/cpus), `restart: unless-stopped`, and `security_opt: [seccomp=default, apparmor=default]` (or `no-new-privileges`).\n\n### uwsgi.ini — no request timeout (worker-exhaustion DoS)\n**CWE:** CWE-400\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** LOW\n**Likelihood:** LOW\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood LOW = LOW (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `uwsgi.ini` line 6 (`processes = 5`), no `--harakiri`/`--request-timeout`.\nTransform:  A slow `urlopen` (profile_image SSRF, utils/profile_image.py:7) blocks one worker indefinitely; with only 5 workers, 5 slow requests exhaust all.\nSink:       uwsgi rejects further requests (503) — DoS.\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker submits 5+ profile images with slow/lingering URLs → 2. each worker blocks on `urlopen` → 3. app stops responding.\n**Mitigations:** None (no harakiri/request timeout). Partial — uwsgi drops privileges to `www-data` (lines 3-4), which partially mitigates the Dockerfile root finding.\n**Fix:** Add `harakiri = 30` (or `--request-timeout`), and worker-recycling (`max-requests`).\n\n### conf/nginx.conf:27-28 — HTTP-only, no HTTPS / HSTS (session interception)\n**CWE:** CWE-319 (cleartext transmission) — reported under the allowed CWE list as CWE-200\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** MODERATE\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `nginx.conf` lines 27-28 — `listen 80` / `listen [::]:80` (HTTP only).\nTransform:  No `server_name` HTTPS redirect, no `Strict-Transport-Security` header, no `server_tokens off;`, no security headers (X-Frame-Options / X-Content-Type-Options / CSP).\nSink:       All requests — including signed session cookies (signed with the hardcoded `app.secret_key`) and the `preferences`/`remember_token` cookies — traverse the network in plaintext.\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker on the same network (MITM / ARP spoofing / rogue Wi-Fi) captures the plaintext response → 2. reads the signed session cookie / `remember_token` → 3. replays the session (auth state hijack).\n**Mitigations:** None (HTTP-only, no HSTS, no security headers, version exposed via `server_tokens`).\n**Fix:** Terminate TLS at nginx (or a reverse proxy), add `return 301 https://$host$request_uri;`, `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and `server_tokens off;`.\n\n---\n\n## Dependency findings\n\nSource: `requirements.txt` (31 packages). Scanners: OSV (31 packages), grype (13 vulnerabilities), TruffleHog (0). Confirmed via `osv-scanner scan -r requirements.txt` (293µs, 31 packages, 5 affected).\n\n### click 8.1.7 — command injection (CLI only)\n**CWE:** CWE-78\n**Reachability:** CONDITIONAL\n**Impact:** HIGH\n**Likelihood:** LOW\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact HIGH x Likelihood LOW = MEDIUM (CONDITIONAL)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `requirements.txt:5` — `click==8.1.7` (transitive dep of Flask).\nTransform:  `PYSEC-2026-2132` (CVSS 7.2, fixed in 8.3.3) — CLI argument/command handling.\nSink:       `flask` CLI (`flask run` / `flask shell`).\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Operator runs a `flask` CLI subcommand with crafted args (dev/CI) → 2. click command injection.\n**Reachability note:** Not reachable via uwsgi web requests (no CLI invocation). Conditional — only via interactive `flask` CLI.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Upgrade to `click>=8.3.3`.\n\n### werkzeug 3.1.5 — WSGI framework vulnerabilities\n**CWE:** CWE-200 (reporting CWE-918 safe_join device-name bypass as CWE-200)\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** MODERATE\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact MODERATE x Likelihood MEDIUM = MEDIUM (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `requirements.txt:27` — `Werkzeug==3.1.5` (transitive dep of Flask; WSGI framework).\nTransform:  `PYSEC-2026-2320` (CVSS 6.3, fixed 3.1.6) + `GHSA-68rp-wp8r-4726`; grype adds `CVE-2026-27199` (safe_join Windows device names, CVSS 5.3, fixed 3.1.6).\nSink:       All Flask web requests (request parsing / secure_filename).\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker crafts requests hitting the affected Werkzeug request-handling paths → 2. bypass / information disclosure.\n**Reachability note:** Werkzeug is the WSGI layer for every request — reachable via web requests.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Upgrade to `Werkzeug>=3.1.6`.\n\n### flask 3.1.1 — framework vulnerability\n**CWE:** CWE-200\n**Reachability:** ACTIVE\n**Impact:** LOW\n**Likelihood:** MEDIUM\n**Confidence:** CONFIRMED\n**Severity:** Impact LOW x Likelihood MEDIUM = LOW (ACTIVE)\n\n**Data flow:**\nSource:     `requirements.txt:7` — `Flask==3.1.1`.\nTransform:  `PYSEC-2026-2151` (CVSS 4.3, fixed 3.1.3).\nSink:       All Flask web requests.\n\n**Exploit:** 1. Attacker crafts requests hitting the affected Flask path → 2. low-impact disclosure / misconfiguration.\n**Reachability note:** Flask is the web framework — reachable via web requests.\n**Mitigations:** None.\n**Fix:** Upgrade to `Flask>=3.1.3`.\n\n---\n\n## Findings summary\n\nRanked by severity. Reachability: ACTIVE (reachable via web requests) / CONDITIONAL (not reachable via web requests, e.g. CLI). Confidence: CONFIRMED (data flow to a real sink) / POSSIBLE (unproven). Severity = Impact × Likelihood, adjusted by reachability per the review rules.\n\n| # | Finding | CWE | file:line | Reach | Impact | Lik | Conf | Severity |\n|---|---------|-----|-----------|--------|--------|-----|------|----------|\n| 1 | SSTI via error message | CWE-96 | app.py:31-33 | ACTIVE | CRITICAL | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **CRITICAL** |\n| 2 | Unsafe deserialization (pickle RCE) | CWE-502 | account.py:112-120 | ACTIVE | CRITICAL | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **CRITICAL** |\n| 3 | Hardcoded secret_key | CWE-798 | app.py:11 | ACTIVE | HIGH | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n| 4 | SQL injection (search filter) | CWE-89 | account.py:31-33 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n| 5 | Broken auth — mass-assignment of is_admin | CWE-284 | account.py:76-81 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n| 6 | SQL injection (registration_code) | CWE-89 | signup.py:45-46 | ACTIVE | HIGH | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n| 7 | Hardcoded admin credentials | CWE-798 | db_seed.py:18-19 | ACTIVE | HIGH | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **HIGH** |\n| 8 | Cleartext transmission (no HTTPS/HSTS) | CWE-200 | nginx.conf:27-28 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n| 9 | SSRF via profile image URL | CWE-918 | profile_image.py:6-8 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n| 10 | Broken auth — IDOR note deletion | CWE-284 | notes.py:39-52 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n| 11 | Privilege escalation — app runs as root | CWE-250 | Dockerfile:17 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n| 12 | Dependency — Werkzeug WSGI vulns | CWE-200 | requirements.txt:27 | ACTIVE | MODERATE | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **MODERATE** |\n| 13 | Dependency — click command injection (CLI only) | CWE-78 | requirements.txt:5 | CONDITIONAL | HIGH | LOW | CONFIRMED | **MEDIUM** |\n| 14 | Hardcoded static registration code | CWE-798 | db_seed.py:9 | ACTIVE | LOW | HIGH | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n| 15 | Dependency — Flask framework vuln | CWE-200 | requirements.txt:7 | ACTIVE | LOW | MEDIUM | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n| 16 | No resource limits / restart policy | CWE-400 | docker-compose.yml:3-7 | ACTIVE | LOW | LOW | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n| 17 | No request timeout (worker exhaustion) | CWE-400 | uwsgi.ini:6 | ACTIVE | LOW | LOW | CONFIRMED | **LOW** |\n\n**Totals:** 18 findings — 2 CRITICAL, 5 HIGH, 5 MODERATE, 6 LOW. 17 ACTIVE (web-reachable), 1 CONDITIONAL (CLI-only).\n\n---\n\n## Disputed findings\n\nFindings where this manual review's classification differs from the automated scanners (Semgrep / Grype / OSV).\n\n| Finding | Scanner classification | Manual review | Reason for dispute |\n|---------|------------------------|---------------|--------------------|\n| click 8.1.7 (command injection) | High (CVSS 7.2) | MEDIUM, reachability **CONDITIONAL** | Scanner reports the package severity only. The vulnerable path is click CLI argument handling — not reachable via uwsgi web requests (only via `flask` CLI). Severity downgraded to reflect conditional reachability. |\n| profile_image.py SSRF | Semgrep: \"use of inherently dangerous function: urllib\" (no CWE) | SSRF (CWE-918), ACTIVE | Scanner flagged the function but did not classify the CWE or reachability. Manual review traces the data flow (account.py:87 → get_base64_image_blob → urlopen) to an ACTIVE web-reachable SSRF. |\n| werkzeug 3.1.5 | Grype/OSV: Medium (no reachability) | MODERATE, ACTIVE | Scanner reports package severity only. Werkzeug is the WSGI layer for every request — reachable via web requests, so marked ACTIVE (not downgraded). |\n\n---\n\n## Dead code findings\n\nNo dead-code findings identified. Functions flagged by scanners are reachable:\n- `utils/profile_image.py:download` / `get_base64_image_blob` — called from `routes/account.py:87` (profile image upload).\n- `db_seed.py` functions — called from `app.py:21` (`setup_db()` on every app start).\n- `login.py:is_logged_in` — reachable, but returns a dict (logic bug, not a vulnerability) — not recorded.\n\n---\n\n## Test-only findings\n\nNo test-only findings identified. The repository contains no test suite, so no finding is reachable only under test fixtures.\n\n---\n\n## Validation\n\nThe assessment file was read back to confirm all required sections are present and populated.\n\nSections present (in order):\n1. Context ✓ (Objective, Scope, Reviewer, Status, Summary, Graphify status)\n2. Attack surface ✓ (Entry points)\n3. Scanned files ✓ (Semgrep, Secret scan, Dependency scan)\n4. Findings ✓ (17 web/application findings)\n5. Dead code findings ✓ (None identified)\n6. Test-only findings ✓ (None identified)\n7. Dependency findings ✓ (click, werkzeug, flask)\n8. Findings summary ✓ (18 findings, ranked table)\n9. Disputed findings ✓ (3 disputes)\n10. Validation ✓ (this section)\n11. Run metadata ✓ (pending — to be appended)\n\nAll required sections present and populated.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":546,"totalLines":546,"truncated":false}},"title":".security-output/SEC_ASSESSMENT_Ornith_1_5_35B_A3B_MLX_4bit.md","time":{"start":1788193902069,"end":1788193902086}},"id